---
import Page from '../../../layouts/Page.astro';
import Terminal from '../../../components/Terminal.astro';
---

<Page path="tasks/branches">
<section id="hw-branches">
  <h2 id="hw-branches"><a href="#hw-branches">Work on two branches at once</a></h2>
  <Terminal kind="shell"><pre><span class="c"># a new universe: branches of its own, named after it</span>
<span class="p">$ </span><span class="in">multiversectl universe new fix/login --about <span class="s">"PR 412 review"</span></span>
<span class="p">$ </span><span class="in">multiversectl universe up fix/login</span>
<span class="p">$ </span><span class="in">multiversectl universe urls fix/login</span>
http://api-fix-login.10-0-0-5.traefik.me
<span class="c"># or number it (leave the number out for the next free one), and say where it starts</span>
<span class="p">$ </span><span class="in">multiversectl universe new 2 --branch feat/invoicing --base api=release/1.2</span></pre></Terminal>
  <p class="muted">A new universe never reuses a branch: if the name is taken in any repository, nothing is created and every clash is listed. A numbered one takes its branch from <code>spec.branch</code>, <code>u&#123;&#123;universe&#125;&#125;</code> by default. Commands accept the name as typed, its directory or its slug. Each universe has its own containers, volumes and hostnames, so the two run side by side.</p>
</section>

<section id="hw-checkout">
  <h2 id="hw-checkout"><a href="#hw-checkout">Review or continue a branch that already exists</a></h2>
  <Terminal kind="shell"><pre><span class="p">$ </span><span class="in">multiversectl universe checkout fix/login</span>           <span class="c"># or with no branch, to pick one</span>
<span class="p">$ </span><span class="in">multiversectl universe checkout fix/login --only-existing</span></pre></Terminal>
  <p>The other way in: it checks out the branch you name in every repository that has it. A repository that lacks it gets a new branch of that name, or with <code>--only-existing</code> stays on its base, detached. It refuses a branch checked out in another universe or in the clone, because git allows a branch in one worktree only, and one no repository has, so a typo never becomes a branch. Removing the universe keeps the branch: only a branch a universe started is ever deleted.</p>
</section>

<section id="hw-repo">
  <h2 id="hw-repo"><a href="#hw-repo">Add a repository to a multiverse</a></h2>
  <Terminal kind="file"><pre><span class="c"># multiverse.yaml</span>
<span class="k">spec</span>:
  <span class="k">repos</span>:
    - <span class="k">name</span>: worker
      <span class="k">url</span>: git@github.com:acme/worker.git</pre></Terminal>
  <p>That is the whole change: a repository no <code>apps</code> entry names gets an app of its own name, its compose files are found by convention, every service they define starts, and the addresses come from their labels. Add an <code>apps</code> entry only to change one of those, or to split the repository into several apps.</p>
  <Terminal kind="shell"><pre><span class="c"># clone the new repo (already-cloned ones are skipped)</span>
<span class="p">$ </span><span class="in">multiversectl catalog sync</span>
<span class="c"># add its worktree to an existing universe; existing checkouts are kept</span>
<span class="p">$ </span><span class="in">multiversectl universe new 3</span>
<span class="p">$ </span><span class="in">multiversectl universe up 3 worker</span></pre></Terminal>
  <p class="muted">A repository you only edit, with no compose file, needs nothing: it is checked out and nothing starts for it.</p>
</section>

<section id="hw-rm">
  <h2 id="hw-rm"><a href="#hw-rm">Remove a universe and its data</a></h2>
  <Terminal kind="shell"><pre><span class="p">$ </span><span class="in">multiversectl universe rm 3</span>
<span class="p">$ </span><span class="in">multiversectl universe rm 3 --yes</span>     <span class="c"># skip the prompt</span></pre></Terminal>
  <p><code>rm</code> lists what it will destroy and asks you to type the number back. It removes the worktrees, the containers and <strong>volumes</strong>, so every database in it, and the directory and state file. The shared clones are untouched. To stop a universe and keep everything, use <code>universe down 3</code>.</p>
  <p>Work that is only in a worktree is a second question, with its own answer: a universe holding uncommitted changes is refused, and named, until <code>--force</code>. Typing a universe's name back says "yes, this universe", not "yes, and the afternoon's work in it". <code>--yes</code> skips the question; it never discards.</p>
  <p>A worktree a container has written into as root holds files you cannot delete, so that one is left where it is: everything else still goes, the universe stays listed, and the command says which paths to remove with <code>sudo</code> and to run <code>rm</code> again to finish. The same goes for a worktree the clone has lost its record of — the directory is taken away even though git will not do it.</p>
</section>

<section id="hw-prune">
  <h2 id="hw-prune"><a href="#hw-prune">Clear out every universe on the machine</a></h2>
  <Terminal kind="shell"><pre><span class="p">$ </span><span class="in">multiversectl system prune --dry-run</span>   <span class="c"># show what it would remove, and stop</span>
<span class="p">$ </span><span class="in">multiversectl system prune</span>             <span class="c"># the idle ones, asking first</span>
<span class="p">$ </span><span class="in">multiversectl system prune --all --yes</span> <span class="c"># everything, running included, no question</span></pre></Terminal>
  <p><code>prune</code> does what <code>rm</code> does, to every universe of every installed multiverse, or only the one named with <code>-m</code>. Two kinds are kept unless you say otherwise, each listed with the reason: one with containers running, until <code>--all</code>; one with uncommitted work, until <code>--force</code>. <code>--yes</code> only skips the question and never discards work.</p>
  <p>It does not touch the clones, the proxy, or the installed multiverses and catalogs. Branches a universe started are deleted only when every commit on them is already on the base or on a remote; one holding anything else is kept and named.</p>
</section>
</Page>
